00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,400
 are going to be deployed in S3 bucket.

16
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,240
 And then we have the runtime environment here, which is going to do the inferencing.

17
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,920
 Now, let's go ahead and see how the requests are processed.

18
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:19,680
 Earlier we discussed there are three ways in which you can make a request to the AWS Bedrock

19
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:24,640
 Service. One is through the SDK. So, maybe you're using AWS Lambda and if you're using Python,

20
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:27,040
 probably you'll use boot of three SDK.

21
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:30,720
 Then through the browser, if you want to access it through the console,

22
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:34,080
 then you can use the browser and then AWS CLI.

23
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:38,480
 Now, let's say you have made an API request with the user input.

24
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,920
 That request is going to go to this AWS Bedrock Service,

25
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,640
 which will be within AWS Bedrock Service account.

26
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,480
 And then it will be routed to this runtime inference.

27
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:54,240
 And what this runtime inference does is it is used to redirect to the right model endpoint

28
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:59,360
 based on the API request. So, within the API request, we discussed earlier here,

29
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,120
 when we were looking at the API structure, we said we're going to provide the model ID,

30
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:08,000
 like here we have an anthropic cloud. So, this runtime inference is going to interpret

31
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,120
 the model ID and then it's going to route it to the right model product account where

32
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,200
 inferencing for that particular model would be done.

33
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:27,120
 So, now, if you're just accessing it from the console, then sometimes it can also store your prompt

34
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:33,040
 history. But nothing would be stored in this AWS Bedrock Service, if the requests are coming from

35
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:39,600
 AWS Lambda, which means the SDK or CLI. So, the reason why AWS has such an architecture

36
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:45,200
 for Amazon Bedrock Service is because a lot of customers are worried that they're sending

37
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:51,280
 their prompt and getting output from these models, which are posted in AWS. So,

38
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:56,960
 there could be scenarios where the model provider might train his model on the customer data.

39
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:04,000
 So, that's why this is hosted on and operated by AWS account so that model providers

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,120
 do not train their models using customer data. So, last thing from the security perspective,

41
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:15,920
 there are two ways in which you can send APA request to this Amazon Bedrock Service.

